---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Workforce Training & Capability System"
  description="Avery Moon designed an adaptive eight-week training, certification, and progression system for a live manufacturing environment."
>
<section class="case-hero case-training">
  <div class="container">
    <a class="case-back" href={`${base}/built-systems/`}>← Built Systems</a>
    <div class="case-kicker">06 / Learning Systems</div>
    <h1>Workforce Training &<br/><em>Capability System</em></h1>
    <p class="case-deck">An adaptive training, certification, and progression framework that turned an undefined post-onboarding experience into a structured path toward capability and advancement.</p>
    <div class="case-role-line"><span>Program Design</span><span>Curriculum Architecture</span><span>Trainer Coordination</span><span>Coaching</span><span>Implementation</span></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-metrics case-metrics-light">
  <div class="container case-metric-grid">
    <div><strong>8</strong><span>Employees in Initial Cohort</span></div>
    <div><strong>8 wks.</strong><span>Planned Curriculum</span></div>
    <div><strong>6–14 wks.</strong><span>Certification Range</span></div>
    <div><strong>120 hrs.</strong><span>Required Visual Training Built In</span></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-section">
  <div class="container case-two-col">
    <div>
      <div class="case-section-no">01</div>
      <div class="eyebrow">The Gap</div>
      <h2>Quality had a certification standard. Operations had no consistent pathway to reach it.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="case-copy">
      <p>After roughly 1.5 days of mandatory HR onboarding, a new employee entered the department without a standardized training plan. Training depended on department, production demand, and whoever happened to be available. Some employees spent weeks shadowing before performing work themselves.</p>
      <p>Supervisors had no shared milestones, assigned trainers, or reliable way to tell whether someone was progressing toward certification. The result was slow qualification, inconsistent capability, employees sitting without clear direction, and uncertainty about test readiness.</p>
      <p class="case-emphasis">I needed to onboard roughly eight people into a department I was still learning myself, so I built the operating system I wished I had inherited.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="training-before">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="eyebrow">The Journey</div>
    <div class="journey-row journey-old">
      <span>HR Onboarding</span><b>→</b><span class="journey-unknown">?</span><b>→</b><span>Shadowing</span><b>→</b><span class="journey-unknown">?</span><b>→</b><span>Quality Exam</span>
    </div>
    <div class="journey-divider"></div>
    <div class="journey-row journey-new">
      <span>Onboarding</span><b>→</b><span>Weekly Curriculum</span><b>→</b><span>Guided Practice</span><b>→</b><span>Feedback</span><b>→</b><span>Mock Assessment</span><b>→</b><span>Gap Closure</span><b>→</b><span>Certification</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-section case-pathway">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="case-section-no">02</div>
    <div class="eyebrow">The 8-Week Pathway</div>
    <h2>Build overlapping capability instead of waiting for one certification at a time.</h2>
    <p class="case-intro">The curriculum intentionally developed multiple competencies in parallel so employees could progress toward visual inspection, pressure testing, dimensional work, and supporting skills without restarting a new training cycle after every qualification.</p>

    <div class="pathway-track reveal">
      <div class="path-stage">
        <i>01</i><strong>Fundamentals</strong>
        <span>Visual Inspection</span><span>Procedures</span><span>Defect Recognition</span>
      </div>
      <div class="path-stage">
        <i>02</i><strong>Applied Skills</strong>
        <span>Pressure Testing</span><span>Blending</span><span>Straightening</span><span>Dimensional Fixtures</span>
      </div>
      <div class="path-stage">
        <i>03</i><strong>Experience Breadth</strong>
        <span>Easy Parts</span><span>Intermediate Parts</span><span>Complex Parts</span>
      </div>
      <div class="path-stage">
        <i>04</i><strong>Readiness</strong>
        <span>Quality Mock Exam</span><span>Targeted Gap Closure</span>
      </div>
      <div class="path-stage">
        <i>05</i><strong>Certification</strong>
        <span>Written Exam</span><span>Practical Exam</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="case-note">The exact weekly rotation varied by employee and trainer capacity. The framework defined what capability had to be built, while the schedule flexed around the realities of a live production environment.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-section case-alt">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="case-section-no">03</div>
    <div class="eyebrow">Built for a Live Operation</div>
    <h2>Structure without rigidity.</h2>
    <div class="decision-grid training-decisions">
      <article>
        <span>01</span><h3>Assign the work, not just the goal.</h3>
        <p>Each employee received a printed Gantt-style schedule showing the week, assigned trainer, focus area, training goals, and supporting procedures so they knew what they were doing and who they were working with each day.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>02</span><h3>Adapt around real constraints.</h3>
        <p>Attendance, trainer availability, and production demand could disrupt the plan. Training shifted to another trainer or to an upstream operation where the employee could practice comparable skills instead of simply waiting.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>03</span><h3>Make trainees participants.</h3>
        <p>Daily or weekly check-ins captured where employees felt confident or needed more practice, then combined that perspective with feedback from experienced operators and leads.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>04</span><h3>Test before the test.</h3>
        <p>Quality performed a mock assessment roughly two weeks before the expected certification date. Any knowledge or capability gaps became the focus of the final training period before the written and practical exams.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-section feedback-section">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="case-section-no">04</div>
    <div class="eyebrow">Closed-Loop Development</div>
    <h2>Training changed based on evidence, not the calendar.</h2>
    <div class="feedback-loop reveal">
      <div>Practice</div><b>→</b>
      <div>Trainee Feedback</div><b>→</b>
      <div>Trainer Observation</div><b>→</b>
      <div>Adjust Curriculum</div><b>→</b>
      <div>Quality Mock</div><b>→</b>
      <div>Targeted Practice</div><b>→</b>
      <div>Certification</div>
      <svg viewBox="0 0 700 90" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M650 64C590 92 90 93 40 63"/><path d="M42 63l17-8M42 63l11 15"/></svg>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-section case-alt">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="case-section-no">05</div>
    <div class="eyebrow">Beyond Certification</div>
    <h2>Capability needed a progression model, too.</h2>
    <div class="progression-layout">
      <div class="level-track">
        <div><small>Level</small><strong>01</strong><span>Foundational proficiency</span></div>
        <b>→</b>
        <div><small>Level</small><strong>02</strong><span>Expanded capability</span></div>
        <b>→</b>
        <div><small>Level</small><strong>03</strong><span>Advanced proficiency</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="progression-copy">
        <p>I also recreated the standards for what employees should know and be capable of before progressing to Level 1, Level 2, and Level 3 within the department. Those levels influence development, raise eligibility, and advancement potential.</p>
        <p>A separate part-experience matrix tracked <strong>Part × Days of Exposure</strong>, helping ensure employees applied acquired skills across varied parts rather than becoming comfortable with only one repeated product.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <a class="related-system" href={`${base}/built-systems/cross-training-matrix/`}>
      <span>Related System</span>
      <strong>Building-Wide Cross-Training & Workforce Capability Dashboard</strong>
      <b>→</b>
    </a>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-outcomes training-outcomes">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="case-section-no light">06</div>
    <div class="eyebrow light">Early Outcomes</div>
    <h2>Eight employees. Eight faster-than-normal certifications.</h2>
    <div class="outcome-grid">
      <div><strong>6–8 weeks</strong><span>Fastest certifications</span></div>
      <div><strong>12–14 weeks</strong><span>Longest certifications in the cohort</span></div>
      <div><strong>~6–8+ months</strong><span>Historically reported certification timelines</span></div>
      <div><strong>Reusable</strong><span>Another supervisor can operate the framework</span></div>
    </div>
    <p>Historical timelines are based on reported departmental norms rather than a formal longitudinal dataset, so I present the comparison conservatively rather than calculating a percentage reduction.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-section amazon-pattern">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="case-section-no">07</div>
    <div class="eyebrow">Pattern Applied Elsewhere</div>
    <h2>A related capability problem at Amazon required a different system.</h2>
    <div class="amazon-compare">
      <div class="amazon-context">
        <span>Amazon RDU1 · Interim Operations Manager</span>
        <strong>~20-person Problem Solve team</strong>
        <p>A shared role concealed uneven capability. Long-tenured Problem Solvers reported gaps across functions such as Piles, ICQA Andons, CDCs, Piles Walk, and Amnesty Blitz.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="amazon-flow">
        <span>Gap Identification</span><b>→</b><span>Monthly Training Plans</span><b>→</b><span>Amazon Learning Modules</span><b>→</b><span>Hands-On Pairing</span><b>→</b><span>Broader Deployment</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="case-note">Amazon supplied the underlying learning content. I built the operating framework that identified gaps, scheduled training around productive assignments, tracked capability, and expanded the team’s ability to flex between Problem Solve functions.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-artifact-section training-artifacts">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="artifact-head">
      <div>
        <div class="case-section-no">08</div>
        <div class="eyebrow">Artifacts</div>
        <h2>Operational tools designed to be used.</h2>
      </div>
      <p>Sanitized versions of the original training materials will be added once exported. The presentation will preserve the practical simplicity of the real tools rather than redesigning them into fictional software.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="artifact-grid">
      <div class="artifact-mini"><div class="mini-gantt"><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i></div><span>Training Timeline</span><small>Week · Trainer · Focus · Goals</small></div>
      <div class="artifact-mini"><div class="mini-matrix"></div><span>Part Experience Matrix</span><small>Part × Days of Exposure</small></div>
      <div class="artifact-mini"><div class="mini-levels"><i>1</i><i>2</i><i>3</i></div><span>Level 1/2/3 Framework</span><small>Progression standards</small></div>
    </div>
    <div class="artifact-caption artifact-caption-wide"><span>Artifact slots reserved</span><strong>Real sanitized materials arriving next</strong><small>Training timeline · Part matrix · Career progression framework · Selected Amazon visual guides</small></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-next">
  <div class="container">
    <span class="eyebrow">Related System</span>
    <a href={`${base}/built-systems/cross-training-matrix/`}>
      <strong>Cross-Training & Workforce Capability Dashboard</strong><span>→</span>
    </a>
  </div>
</section>
</BaseLayout>
